ew tools to get malaria control back on  track  two years ago  who and our partners began a pilot  program to roll out this vaccine in  ghana kenya  and malawi  here is  what we have learned  this vaccine can be delivered through  child health clinics  by ministries of health and readily  reach children at high coverage levels  community demand for the vaccine is  strong  it has broad reach to children including  the most vulnerable  who may not use a bed net thereby  expanding access to preventive measures  to children at risk  it is safe  it significantly reduces  life-threatening severe malaria  and we estimate it to be highly cost  effective  this is a powerful new tool  but like covet 19 vaccines  it's not the only tool  vaccination against malaria does not  replace or reduce the need for other  measures  including bad nets  or seeking care for fever  of course  the key to any public health andover of  this size and scope  is partnership  i thank the children  families and communities who have  participated in this historic pilot  program  i thank the ministries of health of  ghana kenya and malawi for their  leadership in embarking on these pilot  programs  which have continued despite copied 19.  i thank the researchers in africa  who generated the data and insights that  informed this decision  this is a vaccine developed in africa  by african scientists  and we're very proud  i thank  glaxosmithline  and many research partners for creating  the vaccine and puzz for bringing it  from discovery through development with  support from the bill and melinda gates  foundation  and i thank gavi  the global fund and unit aid  who funded the pilot programs and the  evaluations  malaria has been with us for millennia  and the dream of a malaria vaccine has  been a long-held  but unattainable dream  today  the rtss malaria vaccine  more than 30 years in the making  changes the course of public health  history  we still have a very long road to travel  but this is  a long stride down that road  this vaccine is a gift to the world  but its value will be felt most in  africa  because that's where the burden of  malaria is greatest  it's now my pleasure to welcome my  sister and colleague dr shiri moeti  who's regional director for africa to  talk about the difference this vaccine  will make on the continent  dr moyetti you have the floor  thank you very much  dr ted rose director general that was  the director general of the world health  organization hailing and historic day  as he announces the first malaria  vaccine could be rolled out to billions  as experts have given it an approval and  the potential there to save hundreds of  thousands of lives  across the african continent um  let's talk to uh thomas moore who's with  us now thomas this is a huge  announcement from the world health  organization it certainly is you heard  the director general saying that he had  started his scientific career as a  malaria researcher and he had been  hoping all the way along that he would  see the day in his career where there  was a vaccine against this this terrible  disease  and today is that day he said that we  now have a vaccine it's not hugely  effective it prevents around three out  of ten severe cases but so great are the  numbers so severe is the disease that  would still  save the lives of tens of thousands of  people every year and most of these are  children under the age of five one child  every two minutes dies from malaria  almost all of them in sub-saharan africa  now  over the last uh 20 years there's been  this huge effort to try and get bed nets  to people because that is a very cheap  way of preventing malaria but that  effort has stalled and so we're still  seeing large numbers of deaths and  that's why a vaccine is desperately  needed and this this vaccine the squirts  has been tried and tested in three  countries ghana  kenya and and malawi and it has been  shown to be effective there even in  areas where there are a lot of bed nets  being used so now the who is saying  let's roll it out it will need to be  assessed by gavi who are the the main  vaccine funders and once that's secured  then you can expect this to be rapidly  rolled out that's going to be the next  big question isn't it the rollout i mean  we have experience from talking about  that very recently with the uh covert  vaccination and we know about  vaccinations getting to developing  countries can be very difficult those  are the next steps that the world health  organization are going to have to face  absolutely right but what they've done  in kenny malawi and ghana is shown that  this can be given to  young babies this is given from the age  of five months up to 15 18 months  there's four doses  but they can be given at the same time  as measles tb all those other vaccines  that children will have  and people do accept it they see that  this is something that they really want  so uptake has been very high so that's  really encouraging uh that this could be  rolled out further but even more  exciting there's a vaccine which is  being developed by the same oxford  university group that developed the  covet vaccine and it's twice as  effective just think of what that would  mean in terms of rolling back malaria  and that's why that  uh dr tedros said at the end there that  this is a gift to the world that that we  still have a long road to travel but  we're a long way down that road now yeah  and also just an interesting fact the  world has never had a vaccine against a  human parasite not just against malaria  i mean that just puts into context what  a huge  huge  vaccines are generally against viruses  some against bacteria but parasites are  much more complex they can slip away  from the immune system they use a  combination of stealth and deception and  that's how the malaria parasite gets  into the liver this vaccine stops it uh  well it's an incredible feat and a huge  announcement today from the world health  